SECTION 6: RBAC → SERVICE ACCOUNTS → ROLES → ROLEBINDINGS → CLUSTERROLES → CLUSTERROLEBINDINGS → SECURITY BEST PRACTICES
Why RBAC?

RBAC stands for:

Role-Based Access Control

RBAC controls:

Who can access Kubernetes?
What can they do?
Which resources can they access?

Without RBAC:

Everyone can do everything ❌

With RBAC:

Admin -> Full Access
Developer -> Deployment Access
Viewer -> Read Only Access
Real World Example

Suppose your team has:

Pallavi     -> DevOps Engineer
Rahul       -> Developer
Manager     -> Viewer

Requirements:

Pallavi -> Create/Delete Everything
Rahul   -> Deploy Applications Only
Manager -> View Resources Only

RBAC enforces this.

RBAC Components
ServiceAccount
      ↓
Role
      ↓
RoleBinding

Cluster-wide:

ServiceAccount
      ↓
ClusterRole
      ↓
ClusterRoleBinding
Service Accounts
What is a Service Account?

A Service Account is an identity used by Pods to communicate with the Kubernetes API.

Think:

User Account
      ↓
Human

Service Account
      ↓
Application
Default Service Account

Every namespace gets:

default

Check:

kubectl get sa -n ecommerce

Output:

default
Example

Create Service Account

apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount

metadata:
  name: backend-sa
  namespace: ecommerce

Apply:

kubectl apply -f serviceaccount.yaml
Verify
kubectl get sa -n ecommerce
Why Use Service Accounts?

Suppose Backend needs:

Read ConfigMaps
Read Secrets

Instead of giving full admin rights:

Create Service Account
Assign Limited Permissions
Roles
What is a Role?

A Role defines permissions within a namespace.

Example:

Can Read Pods
Can Read Services
Cannot Delete Pods
Role Example
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role

metadata:
  namespace: ecommerce
  name: pod-reader

rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods"]
  verbs: ["get","list","watch"]
Meaning

Allowed:

kubectl get pods
kubectl describe pods

Not Allowed:

kubectl delete pod
Verbs

Common Verbs:

Verb	Meaning
get	Read one resource
list	Read multiple
watch	Monitor changes
create	Create resource
update	Modify resource
patch	Partial update
delete	Delete resource
Interview Question
Q1. What is a Role?

Answer:

A Role defines permissions for resources within a namespace.

RoleBinding
What is RoleBinding?

RoleBinding connects:

Role
     ↓
User/ServiceAccount
Example
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding

metadata:
  name: read-pods-binding
  namespace: ecommerce

subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: backend-sa

roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: pod-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
Flow
backend-sa
      ↓
RoleBinding
      ↓
pod-reader Role
      ↓
Can Read Pods
Verify
kubectl get rolebindings -n ecommerce
Interview Question
Q2. What is RoleBinding?

Answer:

RoleBinding grants the permissions defined in a Role to a User, Group, or ServiceAccount.

ClusterRole
Problem with Role

Role only works:

Inside One Namespace

Example:

ecommerce namespace only
Solution

ClusterRole

Works across:

Entire Cluster
Example
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole

metadata:
  name: node-reader

rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["nodes"]
  verbs: ["get","list"]
